Title: THE LAST SHIFT
Credit: Written by
Author: ClearCut Sample Unit
Draft date: 09/06/2026
Contact: sample script for clearance testing - all characters and events are fictional

FADE IN:

INT. ST. BRENDAN'S MEMORIAL HOSPITAL - CARDIOLOGY WING - NIGHT

Fluorescent hum. A long corridor, half the lights dead. DR. MARCUS WHITFIELD (52), a cardiologist with a runner's build and a tired, expensive haircut, walks fast with a tablet under his arm.

Behind him, NURSE TOVAH RENQUIST (34), sharp-eyed, follows at a distance she thinks is safe.

WHITFIELD
(into his phone)
I told you, the numbers are clean. They'll be clean by morning.

He ends the call. Checks the tablet. Taps. Taps again. A field labeled EJECTION FRACTION changes from 28 to 41.

Tovah sees it. She stops walking.

INT. ST. BRENDAN'S MEMORIAL HOSPITAL - BREAK ROOM - CONTINUOUS

A vending machine glows in the corner. Tovah feeds it coins. A can of NIMBUS COLA drops. She doesn't open it. She just holds it, cold, thinking.

On the TV bolted to the wall: a cable news anchor, muted. A chyron reads SENATOR WARREN TO CHAIR HOSPITAL FUNDING HEARING.

TOVAH
(to herself)
Denver's finest.

She pulls out her phone and takes a photo of the screen behind her: Whitfield's login still open on the break room terminal.

EXT. ST. BRENDAN'S MEMORIAL HOSPITAL - PARKING STRUCTURE - NIGHT

Rain. Tovah hurries to her car, a dented silver Toyota. She parks the Toyota every night in the same corner spot under the one working light.

A figure steps out from behind a pillar. DESMOND VALE (45), the hospital's Chief Financial Officer. Perfect suit. Wrong shoes for rain.

VALE
Long shift.

TOVAH
Last one this week.

VALE
Marcus says you've been reading charts that aren't yours.

TOVAH
Marcus says a lot of things. Some of them are even on the record.

Vale smiles. Hands her a business card. She reads it.

INSERT - BUSINESS CARD

DESMOND VALE, CFO
St. Brendan's Memorial Hospital
303-742-9981

BACK TO SCENE

VALE
Call me before you call anyone else.

He walks away. Tovah looks at the card. Then at the Toyota. Then she gets in and drives.

INT. TOVAH'S APARTMENT - LATER

A small place. Books everywhere. A paperback of MOBY-DICK lies face down on the arm of the couch, spine cracked, she was reading it before all this started.

Tovah drops the business card on the counter next to a takeout menu. She dials a different number from memory.

TOVAH
(into phone)
Reggie. It's me. I need the number for that reporter. The one from the water story.

REGGIE (V.O.)
(filtered)
It's late, Tov.

TOVAH
I know what time it is.

REGGIE (V.O.)
(filtered)
303-555-0142. Don't say I gave it to you.

She writes it on the back of Vale's card.

INT. THE RUSTY ANCHOR - BALTIMORE - NIGHT

Text on screen: BALTIMORE, MARYLAND. THREE DAYS LATER.

A dockside bar. Low ceiling, lower lighting. A jukebox plays something old and slow.

Tovah sits at the far end with a reporter, PRIYA SUNDARAM (40), recorder on the table between them.

PRIYA
So the cardiologist falsifies the ejection fractions, the hospital bills for procedures that were never indicated, and the CFO signs off.

TOVAH
Three years. Maybe more.

PRIYA
And you have the login screenshots.

TOVAH
And the audit trail. Every change is timestamped.

At the bar, two MEN in work jackets pass a paper bag under the counter. Cash goes the other way. Nobody looks. This is that kind of bar.

Priya notices. Tovah doesn't.

PRIYA
Why here?

TOVAH
My brother tends bar here on weekends. Nobody from Denver knows this place exists.

The jukebox changes. Tovah closes her eyes and hums along to the chorus of PURPLE RAIN. She sings one line under her breath, barely audible.

PRIYA
Prince fan.

TOVAH
My mother was. That was her song for bad nights.

INT. ST. BRENDAN'S MEMORIAL HOSPITAL - WHITFIELD'S OFFICE - DAY

Whitfield at his desk. On the wall behind him, framed diplomas. On the desk, an open can of COCA-COLA sweating onto a stack of patient files.

Tovah stands in the doorway. She is not wearing scrubs.

WHITFIELD
You're not on the schedule.

TOVAH
I'm not on staff. As of an hour ago.

WHITFIELD
Then you're trespassing.

TOVAH
Then call security. Or call Vale. Or call whoever you called the night you changed Mrs. Okafor's numbers.

Whitfield stands. Slowly.

WHITFIELD
Careful.

TOVAH
I've been careful for three years. It's your turn.

He lunges for the desk phone. Tovah grabs the Coca-Cola can and throws it. It catches him on the temple, foam and syrup everywhere, and he goes down hard against the filing cabinet.

She steps over him, picks up the tablet, and walks out.

EXT. WASHINGTON, D.C. - PENNSYLVANIA AVENUE - DAY

A wide, bright morning. Tourists. Barricades.

Priya and Tovah stand across the street from 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue, looking at the building, a manila envelope in Priya's hand.

PRIYA
The hearing's at ten. Senator Warren's office wants you in the second row.

TOVAH
I'd rather be in the back.

PRIYA
Then be in the back. Just be there.

Tovah looks at the envelope. Then at the building. Then at her phone, where a text from an unknown number reads: YOU SHOULD HAVE CALLED ME FIRST. - D.V.

She deletes it.

TOVAH
Let's go.

They cross the street.

FADE OUT.

THE END
